.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt WRITE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm write ,
.Nm writev ,
.Nm pwrite ,
.Nm pwrite64 ,
.Nm pwritev
.Nd write bytes to a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn write "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t count"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn pwrite "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t count" "off_t offset"
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn writev "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn pwritev "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt" "off_t offset"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn write
writes up to
.Fa count
bytes from
.Fa buf
to
.Fa fd .
A successful call may write fewer bytes than requested.
The operation depends on the descriptor's backing object.
.Ss Regular files
A regular file is written through its mounted file system at the
open-file description's offset.
A positive result advances that offset by the number of bytes written.
Duplicated descriptors and descriptors inherited through fork share
the offset and status flags.
With
.Dv O_APPEND ,
each write uses the current end of the file, regardless of an intervening seek.
.Pp
A write to a file on a read-only mount fails with
.Er EROFS .
A file attached to a loop device cannot be written directly;
the call fails with
.Er EBUSY .
A write of one or more bytes by an unprivileged process clears setuid and,
when group execute is set, setgid.
.Pp
On ext2, allocated overwrites and contiguous appends can remain in a
bounded mount-wide batch after a successful write.
Reads and stat see accepted bytes and size immediately.
The batch drains at fsync, sync, a bound, an attribute or namespace change,
or the last close of an open-file description.
Sparse writes and hole fills drain before returning.
An I/O error after some bytes were accepted returns their count;
fsync reports the delayed error even after close and reopen.
.Ss Pipes and terminals
A pipe write waits when the pipe is full.
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
it returns the bytes already written, or
.Er EAGAIN
if none fit.
When no reader remains, the calling thread receives
.Dv SIGPIPE ;
the call returns the bytes already written, or
.Er EPIPE
if none were written.
.Pp
Console output passes through the terminal output processor to both
the screen and serial port.
With
.Dv OPOST
and
.Dv ONLCR
set, a newline becomes carriage return followed by newline.
The return value counts input bytes, before this conversion.
Pseudoterminal slave output uses the same processing and enters the
master's 4096-byte ring; bytes that arrive while the ring is full, echo
included, are discarded and still counted as written.
Writing a slave whose master has closed fails with
.Er EIO .
Master writes supply input to the slave's line discipline and may wait
for input space, or fail with
.Er EAGAIN
when nonblocking.
.Pp
A background process writing its controlling terminal receives
.Dv SIGTTOU
when
.Dv TOSTOP
is set.
If the signal is blocked or ignored, the write fails with
.Er EIO ;
otherwise it posts the signal and returns
.Er EINTR .
.Ss Sockets
A stream socket queues bytes for TCP transmission.
If no send space is available, it waits unless
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
is set.
A short return counts only the bytes queued.
A connection no longer able to send returns its recorded error, or
.Er EPIPE
when no error was recorded.
This write path does not generate
.Dv SIGPIPE
for a socket.
.Pp
A datagram socket must have a connected peer.
Each write sends one datagram, with at most 1472 payload bytes.
A broadcast destination requires
.Dv SO_BROADCAST .
Other socket types return
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Ss Devices
Devices with a write operation use that operation.
Whole-disk and partition nodes return
.Er EPERM
on the ordinary kernel; their recovery-kernel writes and writes through
the bounded boot devices require root and sector alignment.
See
.Xr disk 4 .
.Pp
A framebuffer write requires graphics mode and copies at most 64000
bytes to the start of the mode 13h framebuffer.
.Pp
A write of
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
or
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_score_adj
is the file's own, as
.Xr proc 5
describes it, and holds the open file description for its length, as
.Xr lseek 2
holds it to move the offset.
.Ss Gather writes
.Fn writev
writes the buffers described by
.Fa iov
in order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct iovec {
        void *iov_base;
        size_t iov_len;
};
.Ed
.Pp
The vector count must be between 0 and 1024.
Zero-length entries are skipped.
Each nonempty entry is passed to the same operation as
.Fn write .
The call stops at the first short write or error.
.Pp
The kernel reads and checks each vector as it reaches it.
An individual length above 2147483647, or a length that would make the
running byte total exceed that value, stops the call.
An error found after a positive transfer returns the transferred byte
count instead of the error.
With zero vectors, or a readable vector list of zero-length entries
only,
.Fn writev
returns zero without checking the descriptor; an entry that cannot be
read gives
.Er EFAULT .
.Pp
The vector is a sequence of separate writes, not one atomic operation.
On a datagram socket, each nonempty entry sends a separate datagram.
Musl's buffered output uses
.Fn writev .
.Ss Positioned writes
.Fn pwrite
writes up to
.Fa count
bytes at
.Fa offset
of the file
.Fa fd
is open on and leaves the descriptor's offset where it was.
Its checks are those of
.Xr pread 2 ,
in the same order, with a descriptor not open for writing returning
.Er EBADF ;
a directory is never open for writing, so it returns
.Er EBADF
as well.
With
.Dv O_APPEND ,
a regular file is written at its end whatever
.Fa offset
says, as Linux does and POSIX does not.
The regular-file rules of
.Fn write
apply otherwise: a file backing a loop device returns
.Er EBUSY ,
and a write of one or more bytes by an unprivileged process clears the
set-id bits.
A raw disk window is written at
.Fa offset .
The offset the descriptor shares with its duplicates does not change at
any point of the call.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 4 for
.Nm write ,
146 for
.Nm writev
and 181 for
.Nm pwrite64 ,
which takes the descriptor, the buffer and the count in EBX, ECX and
EDX, and the offset's low and high 32 bits in ESI and EDI.
Musl's and GNU libc's
.Fn pwrite
are this call.
.Pp
On x86-64 the numbers are 1 for
.Nm write ,
20 for
.Nm writev ,
18 for
.Nm pwrite64 ,
which takes the offset whole in R10, and 296 for
.Nm pwritev ,
which i386 does not have here.
.Fn pwritev
writes the buffers of
.Fa iov
as
.Fn pwrite
writes one, each at the place the ones before it reached, from
.Fa offset ;
the vector is checked whole before a byte is written, and a short write
ends the call.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the number of bytes written.
.Fn writev
returns the total across the buffers.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/errnoprobe.c
issues system call 146 with standard output as the descriptor,
.Ql 0xFFFFF000
as the vector address, and a vector count of 1.
It requires
.Er EFAULT .
.Pa tests/musl/preadmusl.c
writes a file at offsets with
.Fn pwrite ,
checks that the descriptor's offset does not move, and that
.Dv O_APPEND
writes at the end.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECONNREFUSED
.It Er EACCES
A datagram broadcast was attempted without
.Dv SO_BROADCAST ,
or a loop-control attachment path has a directory the caller cannot
search.
.It Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking pipe, pseudoterminal master, or stream socket cannot
accept bytes without waiting, or a datagram socket cannot acquire
an ephemeral port.
.It Er EBADF
The descriptor is invalid, is not open for writing, has no write operation,
or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
A framebuffer descriptor also gives this error outside graphics mode.
.It Er EBUSY
A regular file backs a loop device, or a loop-control command encounters
an occupied device, an attached file, a mount, a held buffer, or a transfer.
.It Er ECONNREFUSED
A stream connection was refused.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A stream connection was reset.
.It Er EDESTADDRREQ
A datagram socket has no connected peer.
.It Er EFAULT
The buffer or vector list cannot be read as required.
.It Er EFBIG
A file write exceeds the file system's size or addressing limit.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a wait, or a background terminal write posted
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.It Er EINVAL
The vector count is outside 0 through 1024, a vector length exceeds
2147483647, or the next length would overflow the running byte total.
.Fn pwrite
was given a negative offset, a count past 2147483647 or an end past the
signed 64-bit range.
A raw write has an offset or length not divisible by 512.
A device control write has an invalid argument or command.
.It Er EIO
A file system or device write failed, including a write to a protected stick.
A pseudoterminal slave has lost its master.
A background terminal write with
.Dv TOSTOP
also gives this error when
.Dv SIGTTOU
is blocked or ignored.
.It Er ELOOP
A loop-control attachment would exceed the permitted device nesting,
or its path has too many symbolic links.
.It Er EMSGSIZE
A datagram payload exceeds 1472 bytes.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path in a loop-control command exceeds a resolver limit.
.It Er ENETRESET
A stream connection was ended by a network configuration change.
.It Er ENETUNREACH
No interface has a route to the datagram destination.
.It Er ENOBUFS
The local datagram queue is full, or the network device cannot accept
the outgoing frame.
.It Er ENOENT
A loop-control command names an absent file or an unattached device.
.It Er ENOMEM
Resolving or opening a loop-control attachment requires unavailable memory.
.It Er ENOSPC
A file system has insufficient space,
a raw write starts at or crosses the end of its window, or the descriptor
refers to
.Pa /dev/full .
.It Er ENOTDIR
A component of a loop-control attachment path is not a directory.
.It Er ENXIO
A raw disk window is not present.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket type does not support this write path.
.It Er EPERM
A whole-disk or partition node was written on the ordinary kernel,
a raw write was attempted without effective user ID 0,
a loop device node was written directly, or the frame-allocation test
device was written without its boot option.
.It Er EPIPE
A pipe has no reader, or a stream socket can no longer send and has
no recorded error.
.It Er EROFS
The mount is read-only or its type has no write operation.
.It Er ESPIPE
.Fn pwrite
was given a descriptor that cannot seek.
.It Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
A stream connection timed out.
.El
